@@ -802,6 +802,22 @@ You can also be more explicit, by passing in a list of tuples:
 
 This means that the ``author_id`` column should be a foreign key that references the ``id`` column in the ``authors`` table.
 
+To specify ``ON DELETE`` or ``ON UPDATE`` behavior, construct a ``ForeignKey`` object. Both actions default to ``NO ACTION``:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+    from sqlite_utils.db import ForeignKey
+
+    foreign_keys=[
+        ForeignKey(
+            "books", "author_id", "authors", "id",
+            on_delete="CASCADE",
+            on_update="SET NULL",
+        )
+    ]
+
+The same ``on_delete=`` and ``on_update=`` arguments can be used with a compound ``ForeignKey``. These actions are retained when a table is rebuilt using ``.transform()``.
+
 You can leave off the third item in the tuple to have the referenced column automatically set to the primary key of that table. A full example:
 
 .. code-block:: python
@@ -2280,14 +2296,18 @@ Each ``ForeignKey`` has the following attributes:
     A list of the referenced columns.
 ``is_compound``
     ``True`` if this is a compound (multi-column) foreign key.
+``on_delete``
+    The ``ON DELETE`` action, such as ``CASCADE`` or ``SET NULL``. Defaults to ``NO ACTION``.
+``on_update``
+    The ``ON UPDATE`` action, such as ``CASCADE`` or ``SET NULL``. Defaults to ``NO ACTION``.
 
 ``ForeignKey`` was a ``namedtuple`` prior to sqlite-utils 4.0. It is now a dataclass and can no longer be unpacked or indexed as a tuple - access its fields by name instead. See :ref:`upgrading_3_to_4` for details.
 
 ::
 
     >>> db.table("Street_Tree_List").foreign_keys
-    [ForeignKey(table='Street_Tree_List', column='qLegalStatus', other_table='qLegalStatus', other_column='id', columns=['qLegalStatus'], other_columns=['id'], is_compound=False),
-     ForeignKey(table='Street_Tree_List', column='qCareAssistant', other_table='qCareAssistant', other_column='id', columns=['qCareAssistant'], other_columns=['id'], is_compound=False),
+    [ForeignKey(table='Street_Tree_List', column='qLegalStatus', other_table='qLegalStatus', other_column='id', columns=['qLegalStatus'], other_columns=['id'], is_compound=False, on_delete='NO ACTION', on_update='NO ACTION'),
+     ForeignKey(table='Street_Tree_List', column='qCareAssistant', other_table='qCareAssistant', other_column='id', columns=['qCareAssistant'], other_columns=['id'], is_compound=False, on_delete='NO ACTION', on_update='NO ACTION'),
      ...]
 
 Compound foreign keys - defined with ``FOREIGN KEY (col_a, col_b) REFERENCES other(col_a, col_b)`` - are returned as a single ``ForeignKey`` with ``is_compound=True``, ``column`` and ``other_column`` set to ``None``, and the participating columns available in the ``columns`` and ``other_columns`` lists.
